\title{Sharp Silent-Error Constants for Almost-Lossless Compression,\\ and the Necessity of Abstention}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 3, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study almost-lossless source compression by $2$-universal hashing with a decoder that is permitted to abstain, and we separate the error probability into two operationally distinct parts: \emph{loud failure} (the decoder returns nothing usable) and \emph{silent corruption} (the decoder confidently returns a wrong source symbol). For a source $\mu$ on a finite alphabet, a codebook list $l$ of distinct symbols whose complement carries mass at most $\delta$, and a $2$-universal family into $M$ codewords, we determine the exact frontier of constant pairs achievable by derandomization over the key space.

Our results are fourfold. First, an \emph{achievability frontier}: for every $c>1$ a single explicit key attains silent-corruption probability at most $c\,\delta\,|l|/M$ and failure probability at most $\delta + \frac{c}{c-1}|l|/M$ at decoding cost exactly $|l|$; letting $c\downarrow 1$ pushes the silent constant to the first-moment optimum $1$. Second, an \emph{exact optimum}: over the whole admissible region $\frac{1}{c_1}+\frac{1}{c_2}\le 1$ the total-error constant satisfies $c_2+c_1\delta \ge (1+\sqrt\delta)^2$, with equality precisely at $(c_1,c_2)=(1+\delta^{-1/2},\,1+\delta^{1/2})$; the resulting \emph{$\sqrt\delta$-balanced scheme} achieves failure $\le \delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)|l|/M$, silent corruption $\le (\sqrt\delta+\delta)|l|/M$ and total error $\le \delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)^2|l|/M$, with both constants simultaneously optimal as $\delta\to0^+$. Third, \emph{sharpness of the method's boundary}: whenever $K(\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}-1)>1$ there exist bad-key sets of the permitted densities that cover the key space. Fourth, a \emph{scheme-level converse and separation}: for arbitrary encoder--decoder pairs over a code of size $M$, $\Pr[\text{silent}]+\Pr[\text{abstain}] \ge 1 - M\pmax$; hence a decoder that never abstains corrupts silently with probability at least $1-M\pmax$, at least $\tfrac12$ in the compressive regime $M\pmax\le\tfrac12$, while the balanced key attains silent-corruption probability $\le\varepsilon$ for any $\varepsilon$ reachable by the balanced bound. Silent corruption is therefore not an intrinsic cost of compressing below the min-entropy but an artefact of forcing the decoder to commit.

We further prove an $r$-region derandomization principle under $\sum_i 1/c_i\le1$, yielding group-wise (fair) silent-error control with a local per-group bound, and show that products of $2$-universal families are $2$-universal, so that an independently keyed $T$-valued tag divides the silent-error bound by $T$ at unchanged scan cost.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two kinds of being wrong}

Compression below the information content of a source must sometimes be wrong. Classical almost-lossless source coding bounds the total error probability and stops there. Operationally, however, two failure modes with radically different consequences are being conflated.

A \emph{loud failure} is a decoder that declines: it detects that the received codeword is ambiguous or unrecognised and reports so. Downstream systems can retransmit, fall back, or flag. A \emph{silent corruption} is a decoder that confidently emits the wrong symbol. It is indistinguishable from success at the interface and propagates unchecked.

The distinction is exactly the \emph{reject option} / \emph{selective prediction} setting in machine learning, where a predictor may abstain rather than label an input; in coding it is the difference between a detected and an undetected error. This paper develops a sharp theory of the trade-off between the two events in the hashing-based almost-lossless setting, and proves that abstention is not a design convenience but a mathematical necessity.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Frontier achievability} (Theorem~\ref{thm:frontier}): every point $(c,\,c/(c-1))$ of the admissible hyperbola is realised by a single derandomized key, with scan cost exactly $|l|$; the silent constant reaches $1+\varepsilon$ for every $\varepsilon>0$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:nearone}).
  \item \textbf{Exact optimum of the method} (Theorem~\ref{thm:cs}, Proposition~\ref{prop:balpoint}): $c_2+c_1\delta\ge(1+\sqrt\delta)^2$ over the whole admissible region, attained exactly at the balanced point; the $\sqrt\delta$-balanced scheme (Theorem~\ref{thm:balanced}) is the optimum of the method, and an AM--GM computation (Theorem~\ref{thm:amgm}) identifies $\eta=1/\sqrt\delta$ as the exact minimiser of the tunable family.
  \item \textbf{Sharpness of the covering condition} (Theorem~\ref{thm:covering}).
  \item \textbf{Group-wise fairness} (Theorems~\ref{thm:multi} and \ref{thm:groupwise}).
  \item \textbf{Tagging} (Theorems~\ref{thm:prod} and \ref{thm:tagged}).
  \item \textbf{Abstention converse and separation} (Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff}, Corollaries~\ref{cor:commit} and \ref{cor:half}, Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $\XX$ is a finite nonempty alphabet.

\begin{definition}[Source]
A \emph{source} is a probability distribution $\mu$ on $\XX$. For $A\subseteq\XX$ write $\mu(A)=\sum_{x\in A}\mu(x)$, and let $\pmax=\max_{x\in\XX}\mu(x)$ denote the largest atom. The quantity $-\log_2\pmax$ is the \emph{min-entropy} of $\mu$; $M\pmax$ compares the code size to it.
\end{definition}

Mass is monotone, subadditive and normalised; these three facts are all we use.

\begin{definition}[Scheme with abstention]
A \emph{scheme} with finite code space $\CC$ is a pair $\enc:\XX\to\CC$, $\dec:\CC\to\XX\cup\{\bot\}$, where $\bot$ denotes \emph{abstention}. For $x\in\XX$ the scheme \emph{succeeds} at $x$ if $\dec(\enc(x))=x$; \emph{corrupts silently} at $x$ if $\dec(\enc(x))=y$ for some $y\ne x$; and \emph{abstains} at $x$ if $\dec(\enc(x))=\bot$. Write $\Succ,\Sil,\Abs$ for the corresponding subsets. They are pairwise disjoint and cover $\XX$: the \emph{decoding trichotomy}. A scheme \emph{never abstains} if $\Abs=\emptyset$.
\end{definition}

The \emph{failure probability} is $\mu(\XX\setminus\Succ)=\mu(\Sil)+\mu(\Abs)$, the \emph{silent-corruption probability} is $\mu(\Sil)$, and the \emph{abstention probability} is $\mu(\Abs)$.

\begin{definition}[$2$-universal family]
A family $H_1,\dots,H_K:\XX\to\{1,\dots,M\}$ is \emph{$2$-universal} if for all $x\ne y$,
\[
\#\{k : H_k(x)=H_k(y)\}\cdot M \;\le\; K,
\]
i.e.\ a uniformly random key makes any fixed pair collide with probability at most $1/M$. Affine maps $x\mapsto((ax+b)\bmod p)\bmod M$ over a prime field form such a family.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hash scheme with unique-match decoding]\label{def:hs}
Let $l=(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ be a list of \emph{distinct} elements of $\XX$ (the \emph{codebook}) and $h:\XX\to\{1,\dots,M\}$. The scheme $\mathrm{HS}(l,h)$ sets $\enc(x)=h(x)$; and $\dec(i)$ scans $l$ once, collecting $\{x_j\in l:h(x_j)=i\}$, outputting the unique match if the set is a singleton and $\bot$ otherwise. The decoding cost is exactly $n=|l|$ hash evaluations for every codeword: the scan is oblivious and data-independent.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Load, defect, collision set]
Write $L=|l|/M$ (the \emph{load}), and let $\delta$ bound the \emph{codebook defect}: $\mu(\XX\setminus l)\le\delta$. For a key $k$ and $S\subseteq\XX$, say $x$ \emph{collides with $S$ under $k$} if there is $y\in S$ with $y\ne x$ and $H_k(x)=H_k(y)$; write $\Coll_k(S)$ for the set of such $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Success on the codebook]\label{lem:succ}
If $x\in l$ and $x\notin\Coll_k(l)$ then $\mathrm{HS}(l,H_k)$ succeeds at $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$x$ is itself a match for $\enc(x)$ and no other codebook entry matches, so the match set is $\{x\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Silent errors are second-order]\label{lem:sil}
If $\mathrm{HS}(l,H_k)$ corrupts silently at $x$ then $x\notin l$ and $x\in\Coll_k(l)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $x\in l$ then $x$ lies in the match set of $\enc(x)$, so the decoder outputs $x$ or $\bot$; it cannot output $y\ne x$. If it outputs $y\ne x$ then $y\in l$ and $H_k(y)=H_k(x)$, so $x\in\Coll_k(l)$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:sil} is why silent corruption is cheap: it requires the \emph{conjunction} of atypicality and collision, whereas failure requires only their disjunction.

\section{Averaging and thresholded Markov bounds}

\begin{lemma}[First moment]\label{lem:fm}
For a $2$-universal family $H$ and any $S,A\subseteq\XX$,
\[
\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^K \mu\big(A\cap\Coll_k(S)\big) \;\le\; \frac{|S|}{M}\,\mu(A).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Exchange the sums over keys and over $x\in A$; for each $x$ the probability over a uniform key that $x$ collides with some $y\in S$ is at most $|S|/M$ by the union bound and $2$-universality. Weight by $\mu(x)$ and sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Bad keys at level $c$]
For $c>0$, a region $A$ and a codebook set $S$, put
$\mathcal{B}_c(A)=\{k : M\,\mu(A\cap\Coll_k(S)) > c\,|S|\,\mu(A)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Strict Markov bound]\label{lem:markov}
For every $c>0$, $\;|\mathcal{B}_c(A)|\cdot c<K$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Markov's inequality applied to Lemma~\ref{lem:fm} gives $|\mathcal{B}_c(A)|\cdot c\le K$; strictness follows because membership requires a \emph{strict} excess (and the empty case reads $0<K$).
\end{proof}

Strictness is essential: it permits the covering condition to be met with \emph{equality}, which is exactly where the optimum sits.

\begin{proposition}[Two-region derandomization]\label{prop:two}
If $c_1,c_2>0$ and $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}\le1$, some key $k$ is simultaneously $c_2$-good on $\XX$ and $c_1$-good on $\XX\setminus l$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The two bad-key sets have cardinalities $<K/c_2$ and $<K/c_1$; strictness makes their sum $<K$, so their union is not all of $\{1,\dots,K\}$.
\end{proof}

Combining with Lemmas~\ref{lem:succ} and \ref{lem:sil} gives the \emph{tunable scheme}: a single key with
\[
\Pr[\text{failure}]\le\delta+c_2L,\qquad \Pr[\text{silent}]\le c_1\delta L,\qquad \text{cost}=|l|.
\]
Indeed the failure set is contained in $(\XX\setminus l)\cup\Coll_k(l)$, and the silent set in $(\XX\setminus l)\cap\Coll_k(l)$, of mass at most $c_1\frac{|l|}{M}\mu(\XX\setminus l)\le c_1\delta L$.

\section{The exact optimum: Cauchy--Schwarz on the frontier}

The total error of the $(c_1,c_2)$-scheme is $\delta+(c_2+c_1\delta)L$, so the design problem is to minimise $c_2+c_1\delta$ subject to $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}\le1$.

\begin{theorem}[Frontier optimality]\label{thm:cs}
For every $\delta\ge0$ and every $c_1,c_2>0$ with $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}\le1$,
\[
c_2+c_1\delta \;\ge\; \big(1+\sqrt\delta\big)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $s=\sqrt\delta$, so $\delta=s^2$, $s\ge0$. For all nonzero $c_1,c_2$ one has the identity
\[
\big(c_2+c_1s^2\big)\Big(\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}\Big) \;=\; (1+s)^2 + \frac{(c_2-c_1s)^2}{c_1c_2},
\]
verified by clearing denominators and expanding. Since $c_2+c_1s^2>0$ and $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}\le1$, the left-hand side is at most $c_2+c_1s^2$, while the second term on the right is nonnegative as $c_1c_2>0$. Hence $(1+s)^2\le c_2+c_1s^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The excess is a perfect square, so equality forces $c_2=c_1\sqrt\delta$ (the \emph{balanced ray}) and $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}=1$ (the boundary hyperbola); their intersection determines the optimum uniquely.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[The balanced point attains the optimum]\label{prop:balpoint}
For $\delta>0$, at $c_1=1+\delta^{-1/2}$ and $c_2=1+\delta^{1/2}$,
\[
\frac{1}{1+\delta^{-1/2}}+\frac{1}{1+\delta^{1/2}}=1,
\qquad
(1+\sqrt\delta)+\big(1+\delta^{-1/2}\big)\delta=(1+\sqrt\delta)^2 .
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
With $s=\sqrt\delta>0$: $\frac{1}{1+1/s}+\frac{1}{1+s}=\frac{s}{s+1}+\frac{1}{1+s}=1$, and $(1+s)+(1+\tfrac1s)s^2=(1+s)+(s^2+s)=(1+s)^2$.
\end{proof}

A one-parameter reparametrisation of the boundary is $c_1=1+\eta$, $c_2=1+1/\eta$ for $\eta>0$, with total-error functional
\[
E(\eta) \;=\; \delta + \Big(1+\tfrac1\eta\Big)L + (1+\eta)\,\delta\,L .
\]

\begin{theorem}[AM--GM converse within the tunable family]\label{thm:amgm}
For all $\delta,L\ge0$ and $\eta>0$, $E(\eta)\ge\delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)^2L$, with equality at $\eta=1/\sqrt\delta$ when $\delta>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $s=\sqrt\delta$ the exact excess is
\[
\Big(1+\tfrac1\eta\Big)L+(1+\eta)s^2L-(1+s)^2L \;=\; \frac{L}{\eta}(1-\eta s)^2 \;\ge\;0,
\]
vanishing iff $\eta s=1$. Substituting $\eta=1/s$ into $E$ gives $\delta+(1+s)^2L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $\sqrt\delta$-balanced scheme]\label{thm:balanced}
Let $\mu$ be a source, $H$ a $2$-universal family with $K\ge1$ keys into $M\ge1$ codewords, $l$ a codebook of distinct symbols, and $\delta>0$ with $\mu(\XX\setminus l)\le\delta$. Then there is a single key $k$ such that $\mathrm{HS}(l,H_k)$ satisfies
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item $\Pr[\text{failure}] \le \delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)\dfrac{|l|}{M}$;
  \item $\Pr[\text{silent corruption}] \le (\sqrt\delta+\delta)\dfrac{|l|}{M}$;
  \item $\Pr[\text{failure}]+\Pr[\text{silent}] \le \delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)^2\dfrac{|l|}{M}$;
  \item the decoding cost is exactly $|l|$ hash evaluations for every codeword.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the tunable scheme at $\eta=1/\sqrt\delta$, so $c_2=1+\sqrt\delta$ and $c_1=1+\delta^{-1/2}$, noting $c_1\delta=\delta+\delta/\sqrt\delta=\sqrt\delta+\delta$. Item (iii) is the sum of (i) and (ii) with $(1+\sqrt\delta)^2=(1+\sqrt\delta)+(\sqrt\delta+\delta)$. Item (iv) is the obliviousness of the codebook scan of Definition~\ref{def:hs}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Comparison and limits]\label{cor:limits}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
  \item For $\delta\le1$: $1+\sqrt\delta\le2$ and $(1+\sqrt\delta)^2\le4$, so the balanced scheme dominates the symmetric choice $c_1=c_2=2$.
  \item For $0\le\delta\le1$: $\sqrt\delta+\delta\le2\sqrt\delta$, so silent corruption is $O(\sqrt\delta\,|l|/M)$.
  \item As $\delta\to0^+$: $1+\sqrt\delta\to1$ and $\sqrt\delta+\delta\to0$: the failure constant attains the first-moment optimum in the limit while the silent constant vanishes.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

Item (c) cannot be achieved by any \emph{fixed} point of the frontier, since $c_2\to1$ forces $c_1\to\infty$; it is the $\delta$-dependent tuning $c_1=1+\delta^{-1/2}$ that makes both limits happen simultaneously.

\section{The whole frontier is achievable}

\begin{theorem}[Frontier scheme]\label{thm:frontier}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:balanced} with $\delta\ge0$, for every $c>1$ there is a key $k$ with
\[
\Pr[\text{silent}]\le c\,\delta\,\frac{|l|}{M}, \qquad
\Pr[\text{failure}]\le\delta+\frac{c}{c-1}\cdot\frac{|l|}{M},
\]
at decoding cost exactly $|l|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $\eta=c-1>0$ in the tunable scheme: $c_1=1+\eta=c$ and $c_2=1+\frac1\eta=\frac{c}{c-1}$.
\end{proof}

The map $c\mapsto(c,\frac{c}{c-1})$ parametrises the boundary hyperbola for $c>1$; $c=2$ gives the symmetric point, $c=1+\delta^{-1/2}$ the balanced point.

\begin{corollary}[Silent constant near the first-moment optimum]\label{cor:nearone}
For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a key with
\[
\Pr[\text{silent}]\le(1+\varepsilon)\,\delta\,\frac{|l|}{M},
\qquad
\Pr[\text{failure}]\le\delta+\frac{1+\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}\cdot\frac{|l|}{M}.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:frontier} with $c=1+\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Derandomization therefore costs nothing asymptotically in the silent constant; what one pays is the failure constant $\frac{1+\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}\to\infty$. The naive pair $(1,\delta)$ is impossible: $c_2=1$ forces $c_1=\infty$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} forbids $c_2+c_1\delta<(1+\sqrt\delta)^2$.

\section{The covering condition is exactly the boundary of the method}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity of the covering condition]\label{thm:covering}
Let $K\ge1$ and $c_1,c_2>0$ satisfy the density-excess condition
$1<K\big(\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}-1\big)$.
Then there are $B_1,B_2\subseteq\{1,\dots,K\}$ with $|B_1|c_1<K$, $|B_2|c_2<K$ and $B_1\cup B_2=\{1,\dots,K\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $x=K/c_1>0$ and $n=\lceil x\rceil-1$, so $n<x$ and $n\ge x-1$. Take $B_1=\{k:k<n\}$, $B_2=\{k:k\ge n\}$; they cover. For $B_1$: $|B_1|=\min(n,K)\le n$ and $nc_1<xc_1=K$. For $B_2$: if $n\ge K$ then $B_2=\emptyset$ and $0<K$. Otherwise $|B_2|=K-n$, and the hypothesis rearranges to $1<\frac{K}{c_1}+\frac{K}{c_2}-K$; with $n\ge\frac{K}{c_1}-1$ this yields $K-n\le K-\frac{K}{c_1}+1<\frac{K}{c_2}$, i.e.\ $|B_2|c_2<K$.
\end{proof}

Both blocks lie \emph{strictly} below the thresholds that Lemma~\ref{lem:markov} guarantees for genuine bad-key sets, yet they exhaust the key space. No argument that knows only the two Markov densities can produce a good key when $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}>1$ and $K$ is large enough. The covering condition is precisely the edge of the counting method, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} the value $(1+\sqrt\delta)^2$ is precisely the best point on it.

\section{Many regions: fair, group-wise silent-error control}

\begin{theorem}[$r$-region derandomization by fractional covering]\label{thm:multi}
Let $A_i\subseteq\XX$ for $i$ in a finite index set $T$, and let $c_i>0$ with $\sum_{i\in T}\frac1{c_i}\le1$. Then a single key $k$ satisfies, for every $i\in T$,
\[
M\,\mu\big(A_i\cap\Coll_k(S)\big) \;\le\; c_i\,|S|\,\mu(A_i).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each bad-key set has cardinality $<K/c_i$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:markov}. If $T=\emptyset$ any key works. Otherwise subadditivity of cardinality over unions and strict summation give $|\bigcup_i\mathcal{B}_{c_i}(A_i)|<\sum_iK/c_i=K\sum_i1/c_i\le K$, so some key avoids every bad set.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Group-wise silent-error control]\label{thm:groupwise}
Let $G_1,\dots,G_r\subseteq\XX$ be arbitrary, possibly overlapping, subpopulations. Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:balanced} there is a single key $k$ with
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item global failure probability $\le \delta+(r+1)\dfrac{|l|}{M}$;
  \item for every group $g$, $\;\mu(\Sil\cap G_g) \le (r+1)\,\mu(G_g\setminus l)\,\dfrac{|l|}{M}$;
  \item decoding cost exactly $|l|$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:multi} to the $r+1$ regions $\XX$ and $(\XX\setminus l)\cap G_g$, $g=1,\dots,r$, all with the uniform threshold $c=r+1$; the covering sum is $\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}\frac{1}{r+1}=1$, exactly admissible. On $\XX$ this bounds $\mu(\Coll_k(l))\le(r+1)|l|/M$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:succ} the failure set lies in $(\XX\setminus l)\cup\Coll_k(l)$, giving (i). On $(\XX\setminus l)\cap G_g$, Lemma~\ref{lem:sil} gives $\Sil\cap G_g\subseteq(\XX\setminus l)\cap G_g\cap\Coll_k(l)$, giving (ii).
\end{proof}

The per-group bound is \emph{local}: it is driven by $\mu(G_g\setminus l)$, not by the worst group or the global defect. Since $\mu(G_g\setminus l)\le\delta$, each group's bound is at most $(r+1)\delta|l|/M$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:groupwise} is never weaker than the aggregate statement and is strictly stronger for well-covered groups.

\begin{corollary}[Individual coverage defects]
If $\mu(G_g\setminus l)\le\delta_g$ for each $g$, the same key gives $\mu(\Sil\cap G_g)\le(r+1)\,\delta_g\,\frac{|l|}{M}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Tagged codewords: exponential suppression at no extra scan cost}

\begin{theorem}[Products of $2$-universal families are $2$-universal]\label{thm:prod}
Let $H_1,\dots,H_K:\XX\to\{1,\dots,M\}$ and $G_1,\dots,G_{K'}:\XX\to\{1,\dots,T\}$ be $2$-universal. Define the \emph{tagged family} on $\{1,\dots,KK'\}$ by
\[
\widetilde H_{(k,k')}(x) \;=\; \big(H_k(x),G_{k'}(x)\big)\in\{1,\dots,M\}\times\{1,\dots,T\}\cong\{1,\dots,MT\}.
\]
Then $\widetilde H$ is $2$-universal into $MT$ codewords.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $x\ne y$, $\widetilde H_{(k,k')}(x)=\widetilde H_{(k,k')}(y)$ iff $H_k(x)=H_k(y)$ and $G_{k'}(x)=G_{k'}(y)$; hence the bad-key set of the product is the Cartesian product of the two bad-key sets, of cardinality $NN'$ with $NM\le K$ and $N'T\le K'$. Multiplying these nonnegative inequalities gives $(NN')(MT)\le KK'$.
\end{proof}

This is not a relabelling of ``use $MT$ codewords'': the tag is produced by an independent key, so the key space grows multiplicatively while the universality constant is exactly $1/(MT)$.

\begin{theorem}[Tagged balanced scheme]\label{thm:tagged}
With $\delta>0$ and $\mu(\XX\setminus l)\le\delta$, there is a single key pair $(k,k')$ whose tagged scheme achieves
\[
\Pr[\text{silent}]\le(\sqrt\delta+\delta)\frac{|l|}{MT},\qquad
\Pr[\text{failure}]\le\delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)\frac{|l|}{MT},
\]
with decoding cost still exactly $|l|$ evaluations of the tagged hash.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:balanced} to the tagged family, $2$-universal into $MT$ codewords by Theorem~\ref{thm:prod}.
\end{proof}

With $T=2^t$ the silent-error bound improves by $2^{-t}$: silent corruption is exponentially rare in the tag length, at unchanged scan cost. The tag converts would-be silent corruptions into detected failures --- it moves mass from the ``confident lie'' bucket into the ``I don't know'' bucket, which the next section shows is the only place it can go.

\section{Abstention is necessary: a converse and a separation}

\begin{lemma}[Pigeonhole on the success set]\label{lem:pigeon}
For any scheme with code space $\CC$, $\mu(\Succ)\le|\CC|\cdot\pmax$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
On $\Succ$ the encoder is injective: if $\enc(x)=\enc(x')$ with $x,x'\in\Succ$ then $x=\dec(\enc(x))=\dec(\enc(x'))=x'$. Hence $|\Succ|\le|\CC|$ and each element carries mass at most $\pmax$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Abstention / silent-corruption trade-off]\label{thm:tradeoff}
For \emph{every} scheme with code space of size $M$,
\[
\mu(\Sil)+\mu(\Abs) \;\ge\; 1-M\pmax .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the decoding trichotomy $\XX=\Succ\cup\Sil\cup\Abs$, so $1=\mu(\XX)\le\mu(\Succ)+\mu(\Sil)+\mu(\Abs)$ by monotonicity and subadditivity. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:pigeon} and rearrange.
\end{proof}

This is a conservation law: below the min-entropy of the source, the mass $1-M\pmax$ that the code cannot carry must be spent on silent corruption or abstention.

\begin{corollary}[Committing decoders must lie]\label{cor:commit}
If a scheme never abstains then $\mu(\Sil)\ge1-M\pmax$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Half the mass]\label{cor:half}
If in addition $M\pmax\le\frac12$ --- exactly the regime in which compression is actually happening --- then a committing decoder satisfies $\mu(\Sil)\ge\frac12$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The abstention separation]\label{thm:sep}
Fix a source $\mu$, a $2$-universal family into $M$ codewords, a codebook $l$ of distinct symbols with $\mu(\XX\setminus l)\le\delta$ for some $\delta>0$, a compressive code size $M\pmax\le\frac12$, and a target level $\varepsilon$ with $(\sqrt\delta+\delta)\frac{|l|}{M}\le\varepsilon$. Then both hold:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{(achievability, with abstention)} there is a key $k$ with $\mu(\Sil)\le\varepsilon$, $\mu(\XX\setminus\Succ)\le\delta+(1+\sqrt\delta)\frac{|l|}{M}$ and cost exactly $|l|$;
  \item \textbf{(converse, without abstention)} \emph{every} scheme over the same code space whose decoder never abstains satisfies $\mu(\Sil)\ge\frac12$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first part is Theorem~\ref{thm:balanced} together with $(\sqrt\delta+\delta)\frac{|l|}{M}\le\varepsilon$; the second is Corollary~\ref{cor:half}.
\end{proof}

The gap in silent-corruption probability between the two decoder classes is $\frac12-\varepsilon$, and $\varepsilon$ can be driven to $0$ by increasing $M$ while remaining in the compressive regime; the ratio is therefore unbounded. Silent corruption is not an intrinsic cost of compressing below the min-entropy: it is entirely an artefact of forcing the decoder to answer.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Key selection by bad-set elimination.} Given $\mu$, $H$, $l$ and thresholds $(c_1,c_2)$ with $\frac1{c_1}+\frac1{c_2}\le1$: for each key $k$ compute $\mu(\Coll_k(l))$ and $\mu((\XX\setminus l)\cap\Coll_k(l))$, discard $k$ if either exceeds its threshold times the corresponding first moment, and return any survivor; Proposition~\ref{prop:two} guarantees one exists. The naive cost is $O(K|\XX||l|)$ hash evaluations, reducible to $O(K(|\XX|+M))$ by bucketing the codebook per key.

\paragraph{(B) Balanced tuning.} Given $\delta$, set $c_1=1+\delta^{-1/2}$, $c_2=1+\delta^{1/2}$ and run (A); by Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} and Proposition~\ref{prop:balpoint} this is the optimal admissible pair.

\paragraph{(C) Unique-match decoding.} Scan $l$ once maintaining a candidate and a match counter; return the candidate iff the counter is exactly $1$, else $\bot$. Cost exactly $|l|$ hash evaluations, data-independent --- a fixed, predictable budget.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Selective prediction.} Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff} is a hard lower bound on the price of a ``no-abstain'' policy in any system mapping inputs to a representation smaller than their information content; Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} says the reject option buys an unbounded factor in confident-error rate.

\paragraph{Compressed caches and retrieval.} Hash-bucketed key--value stores are exactly Definition~\ref{def:hs}. The balanced key gives an explicit, non-adaptive, fixed-cost scan with silent-collision rate $(\sqrt\delta+\delta)|l|/M$, improved by $2^{-t}$ with $t$ tag bits (Theorem~\ref{thm:tagged}).

\paragraph{Fairness auditing under compression.} Theorem~\ref{thm:groupwise} answers the objection that an aggregate silent-error certificate hides subgroup harm: for $r$ audited groups one key certifies each group locally at cost factor $r+1$.

\paragraph{Error detection in storage and transport.} The product principle of Theorem~\ref{thm:prod} formalises why an independent checksum multiplies detection power.

\section{Discussion}

Three boundaries have been located precisely. \emph{The optimum of the method}: Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} pins the best achievable total-error constant at $(1+\sqrt\delta)^2$, attained by the balanced point; the ideal pair $(1,\delta)$ is unreachable since $c_2=1$ forces $c_1=\infty$. \emph{The boundary of the method}: Theorem~\ref{thm:covering} shows fractional covering is exactly where union-bound derandomization stops, so any improvement must use structure beyond the two Markov densities. \emph{The boundary of decoding}: Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff} is a converse for schemes, not merely for the method, and is the right primitive for a converse theory of silent errors.

A caveat: the constant $\frac12$ in Corollary~\ref{cor:half} is not claimed sharp for every source, only for the regime $M\pmax\le\frac12$; the exact statement $\mu(\Sil)\ge1-M\pmax$ degrades gracefully as the code approaches the min-entropy and becomes vacuous above it, as it must.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{A converse frontier for arbitrary schemes.} Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff} constrains the pair $(\mu(\Sil),\mu(\Abs))$ by one linear inequality; the natural next object is the full achievable region of (silent, abstain, cost) for arbitrary schemes, and a matching converse to the $(1+\sqrt\delta)^2$ frontier at the level of schemes.

\paragraph{Beyond union bounds.} Since Theorem~\ref{thm:covering} marks the exact boundary of fractional covering, improvements must exploit second-moment concentration over the key space, explicit algebraic families with better-than-universal collision behaviour, or list-decoding relaxations.

\paragraph{Sharper group-wise constants.} Non-uniform thresholds optimised against the group masses $\mu(G_g\setminus l)$ subject to $\sum_i1/c_i\le1$ should give a weighted frontier analogous to Theorem~\ref{thm:cs}.

\paragraph{Tag-length optimisation.} The trade-off $\big(\log_2(MT),\,(\sqrt\delta+\delta)|l|/(MT)\big)$ should be optimised against an explicit cost model for downstream silent errors.

\paragraph{Adaptive codebooks.} Jointly optimising the codebook against the balanced constants --- enlarging $l$ shrinks $\delta$ but raises $L=|l|/M$ --- is the natural next design problem.

